staging: media: ipu7: Survive a failed wake and an unbind

Unbinding the PCI driver frees the ISYS and PSYS MMUs before deleting
their devices, and the PSYS driver frees its boot config through its
MMU as it unbinds, so it follows a NULL mapping and the kernel oopses
holding the IPU's device lock; suspend and reboot then hang on that
lock. Unbind both drivers first, while their MMUs are still there.

Runtime resume returns ipu_buttress_powerup()'s error as it is, and
runtime PM keeps any error but -EAGAIN and -EBUSY, so one power-up
that times out leaves the device refusing every wake until the next
boot. Report it as -EBUSY, as a driver resume that fails already is.

Export ipu_buttress_authenticate() so the PSYS driver can authenticate
the firmware on a wake after a system resume that couldn't.

--- a/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7.c
@@ -2629,6 +2629,18 @@
 {
 	struct ipu7_device *isp = pci_get_drvdata(pdev);
 
+	/*
+	 * The ISYS and PSYS drivers free DMA through their MMUs as they
+	 * unbind, so unbind them while the MMUs are still there: deleting
+	 * the devices after ipu7_mmu_cleanup() has them free through a
+	 * mapping that is gone, and the kernel oopses holding this
+	 * device's lock.
+	 */
+	if (!IS_ERR_OR_NULL(isp->psys))
+		device_release_driver(&isp->psys->auxdev.dev);
+	if (!IS_ERR_OR_NULL(isp->isys))
+		device_release_driver(&isp->isys->auxdev.dev);
+
 	if (!IS_ERR_OR_NULL(isp->isys) && isp->isys->fw_sgt.nents)
 		ipu7_unmap_fw_code_region(isp->isys);
 	if (!IS_ERR_OR_NULL(isp->psys) && isp->psys->fw_sgt.nents)
--- a/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7-buttress.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7-buttress.c
@@ -945,6 +945,7 @@
 
 	return ret;
 }
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_NS_GPL(ipu_buttress_authenticate, "INTEL_IPU7");
 
 static int ipu_buttress_send_tsc_request(struct ipu7_device *isp)
 {
--- a/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7-bus.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/ipu7/ipu7-bus.c
@@ -47,9 +47,15 @@
 	struct ipu7_bus_device *adev = to_ipu7_bus_device(dev);
 	int ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * Runtime PM keeps any error but busy and refuses every wake after
+	 * it, so a power-up that timed out once would leave the device
+	 * failed until the next boot. Call it busy, as a driver that fails
+	 * to resume is below, so the next wake tries again.
+	 */
 	ret = ipu_buttress_powerup(dev, adev->ctrl);
 	if (ret)
-		return ret;
+		return -EBUSY;
 
 	ret = pm_generic_runtime_resume(dev);
 	if (ret)
